## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are very promising adsorbents with tunable pore size, large pore volume, high specific surface area, flexible and case-specific tailoring of basic molecular architecture and functional groups, and large and selective gas adsorption capacities for small gas molecules. A large volume of MOFs have been reported in the literature, most of them were synthesized and decorated accordingly with an aim towards maximizing the hydrogen adsorption equilibrium amount in order to boost the hydrogen storage capacity. The zinc-based MOFs, also known as the isoreticular metal–organic frameworks (IRMOFs), were synthesized originally by Yaghi’s group, and later modified by many other researchers [1–10]. Apart from the zinc-based MOFs, several other metals including Cu [11], Cr [12], Al [13], Fe [14], Sc [15], Mn [16] or $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based [17] MOFs were also synthesized and evaluated for gas adsorption. Irrespective of metal types, all the MOFs were composed of metal centers linked with each other by organic molecules, more commonly known as organic linkers thereby making a 2D or 3D network of molecules within which the gas can be adsorbed. Despite the synthesis of several other metal-based MOFs, two Zn-MOFs, MOF-5 and MOF-177 were widely investigated for adsorption of different gas molecules.
Apart from hydrogen, adsorption of several other gases was also examined on many types of MOFs. Saha et al. [18] measured the equilibrium and kinetics of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}0$ onto MOF-5 and MOF-177. Saha and Deng [19] performed equilibrium and kinetics study of carbon monoxide over MOF-5 and MOF-177. Millward and Yaghi [20] measured ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake over various types of IRMOFs, and MOF-177 was proved to be the best adsorbent for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ at elevated pressures and ambient temperatures. Chowdhury et al. [21] performed $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $S\mathrm{F}_{6}$ adsorption on MIL-101. Britt et al. [22] employed Mg-MOF-74 for selective separation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . In another work, Britt et al. [23] used several MOFs including MOF-5, MOF-177, MOF-74, MOF-199, IRMOF-3 and IRMOF-62 for the adsorptive removal of several pollutant gases including dichloromethane, tetrahydrothiphene, ethylene oxide, benzene, $S0_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ .
Ammonia is being removed from air in air purification processes. Several carbon-based adsorbents were studied for the adsorptive removal of ammonia [24–26]. Adsorption of a strong reacting gas like ammonia on metal–organic frameworks, especially the IRMOFs, was hardly investigated and its effects on MOF framework stability is unknown. Petit and Bandosz [27] measured the adsorption breakthrough curves of ammonia from a mixed bed consisting of MOF-5 and graphitic oxide, they clearly mentioned that ammonia is detrimental to the MOF-5 frameworks as it behaves like the water molecule to form a hydrogen bonding with the $\mathtt{Z n_{4}O}$ Unit, however they did not report the ammonia adsorption equilibrium and kinetics data. The very low specific surface area $(798\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ of the pristine MOF-5 makes it very difficult to draw a definite conclusion about the ammonia effects on MOF-5 structure [27]. In an earlier work, Britt et al. [23] measured adsorption of ammonia on several MOFs, but they did not verify the aftermath of the framework structure.
The main objective of this work is to determine the ammonia adsorption equilibrium and kinetics on both MOF-5 and MOF177, and to quantify the structural and other property changes of MOF frameworks after ammonia adsorption on the MOFs. Ammonia adsorption equilibrium and kinetics were measured volumetrically at an ambient temperature (298 K) and ammonia pressure up to 800 torr. After the ammonia adsorption, the MOF framework structure was examined with $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption and desorption, X-ray diffraction, FT-IR and Raman spectroscopy to monitor the MOF framework structural and other property changes.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Synthesis of MOF-5 and MOF-177
The MOF-5 and MOF-177 samples were synthesized in our laboratory following the synthesis procedures reported in our previous publications [7,8]. A brief description of the synthesis procedures is given below. For MOF-5 synthesis, all the chemicals were purchased from Fisher Scientific, and they are of the highest available commercial purity $99+\%$ , except zinc nitrate hexahydrate of $98\%$ purity). $0.832{\mathrm{g}}$ of zinc nitrate hexahydrate and $0.176{\mathrm{g}}$ of benzene dicarboxylic acid were dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of $N,N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -diethylformamide (DEF) under constant agitation at ambient conditions. The resulting mixture was first degassed thrice using the freeze– pump-thaw method, and then put to $20~\mathrm{mL}$ vials for reaction. The capped vials were immediately put in an oven at $(85-90^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}})$ for crystallization for about $24\mathrm{h}$ . At the end of the crystallization step, clear golden crystals of MOF-5 emerged from the wall and base of the vials. The MOF-5 crystals were separated from the reaction solution, washed with DMF to remove the unreacted zinc nitrate, and followed by purification in chloroform. The chloroform purification was performed by adding chloroform into $20~\mathrm{mL}$ vials containing the raw MOF-5 crystals. The vials were capped tightly and put back in the oven at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for another 3 days. Solvent in the vials was replenished with fresh chloroform every day. Because MOF-5 crystals are very susceptible to moisture and air, they have to be stored in chloroform or under a vacuum in a Schlenk flask.
The synthesis of MOF-177 can be divided into two steps: synthesis of the benzene tribenzoate (BTB) ligand and formation of MOF-177 crystals. The BTB ligand was synthesized in our laboratory following the procedures reported by Furukawa et al. [28]. To produce MOF-177, $0.32{\mathrm{g}}$ of zinc nitrate hexahydrate and $0.07{\mathrm{g}}$ of BTB ligand were dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of dimethyl formamide. The mixture was degassed three times using the freeze-pump thaw method and then stored in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ reaction vial that was capped tightly. The vial was then put in an oven at $67^{\circ}C$ for 10 days. At the end of this step, clear and transparent MOF-177 formed and became visible in the wall as well as on the base of the vial. The vial was then removed from the oven, decanted, and washed with dimethyl formamide to remove the unreacted zinc nitrate. The raw MOF-177 crystals were then purified by a similar chloroform treatment process employed for MOF-5 purification and stored in chloroform or in a Schlenk flask under vacuum because MOF-177 is also susceptible to degradation in humid air.
## 2.2. Materials characterization
All the material characterizations including pore texture properties by $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption study at 77 K, FT-IR and Raman spectral analysis and X-ray diffraction study were performed before and after $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ adsorption in order to compare any changes in the structure of MOF-5 and MOF-177.
Nitrogen adsorption desorption study was performed in Micromeritics ASAP 2020 surface area and porosity analyzer up to
800 torr pressure and $77\mathrm{K}$ temperature. Specific surface areas including BET and Langmuir surface area, pore width and pore volume were calculated directly from the adsorption–desorption plot by the instrument’s built-in software. FT-IR spectrum was taken in Perkin-Elmer Spectrum D instrument with ATR facility by utilizing a zinc-selenide reflector. Raman spectral lines were achieved in a Rennishaw in-via Raman microscope incorporated with a $633{\mathrm{nm}}$ He–Ne laser source. X-ray patterns were generated with a Rigaku Miniflex-II table-top X-ray diffractrometer with zero background sample holder. The X-ray was generated using Cu-Ka emission $(\lambda=1.54056\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ with $30\mathrm{kV}$ potential difference and $15\mathrm{mA}$ filament current. Theta-calibration and generation of a default FWHM curve of the $\mathsf{X}.$ -ray diffractrometer were performed using an NIST standard silicon powder sample. For each sample, the XRD scan was performed from $3^{\circ}$ to $75^{\circ}$ with $0.03^{\circ}$ width and 1 s count time. The goniometer was attached with a $\mathsf{K}\upbeta$ filter. The XRD data were processed using commercial software Jade ${8}^{+}$ developed by Materials Data Inc., Livermore, CA, USA.
## 2.3. Ammonia adsorption measurements
Ammonia adsorption measurements were also performed in Micromeritics ASAP 2020 surface and porosity analyzer up to $800\mathrm{mm}$ torr pressure and $298\mathrm{K}$ temperature. Commercially available highest purity ammonia was introduced into the adsorption system at a given pressure, and the changes of gas pressure with time were recorded and converted into the transient adsorption amount as a function of time. The transient adsorption uptakes generated the adsorption kinetics, and the final adsorption amount at the terminal pressure determined the adsorption equilibrium amount at a given pressure.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Ammonia adsorption
## 3.1.1. Adsorption equilibrium
Adsorption isotherms of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ on MOF-5 and MOF-177 at 298 K and pressure up to 800 torr are plotted in Fig. 1. It is observed that the maximum adsorption amount at the terminal gas pressure is about $12.2\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g}$ for both MOF-5 and MOF-177. It is also noticeable that the final adsorption amount measured in this study is quite higher than the breakthrough capacity for MOF-5 reported elsewhere [23,27], which is around $6.6\:\mathrm{mg/g}$ . The $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ adsorption amount for MOF-177 is higher than MOF-5 at ammonia pressures below ${\sim}150$ torr, after which the two isotherms followed the same path. It is expected that the physical adsorption of ammonia on MOF-177 is higher than that on MOF-5 because MOF-177 has a much higher specific surface area and larger pore volume. So it is reasonable to assume that physical adsorption of ammonia on MOF-5 and MOF-177 occurred at ammonia pressures below $\mathord{\sim}150$ torr. Because adsorption of ammonia on MOF-5 started to catch up after $\sim150$ torr, we suspect chemical reaction between ammonia and MOFs started to take over. The independence of terminal adsorption amounts with respect to specific surface area and pore volume of the two MOFs is probably attributed to the chemical reaction between ammonia and the MOFs, which will be discussed in details in Section 2.2. The higher ammonia uptake amount by MOF-5 in this work compared to the previous study is most probably caused by the longer exposure of $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ with the MOF-5 and MOF-177 during the kinetic measurements that facilitated the prolonged time of reaction of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ with the MOFs. The very low specific surface area of the pristine MOF-5 $(798\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ could also be partly responsible for the lower ammonia uptake in their study.
## 3.1.2. Adsorption kinetics
Adsorption kinetics data of ammonia on these two MOFs were collected at the same time when the isotherms were measured at $298\mathrm{K}$ in the Micromeritics ASAP 2020 adsorption unit. Kinetic uptake curves at five different pressure levels are shown in Fig. 2a and b for MOF-5 and MOF-177, respectively. It is observed that MOF-5 and MOF-177 reached the adsorption saturation level within $90\:s$ . However, the kinetics of MOF-177 appeared to be slightly faster than MOF-5, which can be attributed to the larger pore opening of MOF-177. A classical diffusion model was applied to correlate the adsorption kinetics data and to extract the diffusion time constants for ammonia in these adsorbents. The fractional adsorption uptake $\left(m_{t}/\mathrm{m}_{\infty}\right)$ can be correlated with the diffusion time constant by the following equation if the fractional adsorption uptake is greater than $70\%$ [29].
$$
1-\frac{m_{t}}{m_{\infty}}=\frac{6}{\pi^{2}}\exp\left(\frac{-\pi^{2}D_{c}t}{r_{c}^{2}}\right)
$$
where $m_{t}$ is the mass is adsorbed at any time t, $m_{\alpha}$ is the final or maximum mass adsorbed, $D_{c}$ is intracrystalline diffusivity and $r_{c}$ is the radius of equivalent sphere of adsorbent crystals.
The diffusion time constants $(D_{c}/r_{c}^{2},s^{-1})$ were calculated from the slope of a linear plot of $\ln(1-(m_{t}/m_{\infty}))$ versus $t$ (time) at a given ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ pressure. Only data points with $(m_{t}/m_{\infty})$ greater than $70\%$ and less than $99\%$ were used for estimating the diffusion time constants which is given in Fig. 3. It is observed that the diffusion time constants decrease with increase in ammonia pressure for both MOF-5 and MOF-177. The decrease in diffusion time could be contributed to the accumulation of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ molecules in the framework of MOF-5 and MOF-177 thereby clogging the pore space.
## 3.2. Pore texture
The pore textural properties including Langmuir and BET specific surface areas, and pore volume were calculated from the MOFs before and after ammonia adsorption by $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption measurements at $77\mathrm{K}.$ The ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake of initial MOF-5 and MOF177 were 900 and $1700\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{STP}/\mathrm{g}$ respectively, which resulted in high specific surface areas of $3917\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Langmuir) and $2449\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (BET) for MOF-5, and $5994\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Langmuir) and $3275\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (BET) for MOF-177. The pore textural properties of these two MOFs before and after ammonia adsorption were summarized in Table 1. The subscript ‘i’ stands for initial or fresh MOF and the subscript $\cdot f$ stands for final MOF after ammonia adsorption. After ammonia adsorption, the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ uptake amounts decrease drastically to 10 and $5\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{STP}{/}\mathrm{g}$ for MOF-5 and MOF-177 respectively. The decrease in ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake causes the massive decline in the pore texture properties of the resultant MOFs; $60\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Langmuir) and $10\mathrm{m}^{\bar{2}}/\mathrm{g}$ (BET) for MOF-5; and $15\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Langmuir) and $4\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (BET) for MOF-177. Besides the specific surface areas, there is a severe reduction in pore volume of both types of MOFs; the pore volume was reduced from 1.39 to $0.014\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ for MOF-5 and 2.65 to $0.008\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ for MOF-177. From the pore texture properties, it is reasonable to conclude that ammonia adsorption on MOFs causes a massive damage to the frameworks of both MOFs. The reduction of BET surface area and pore volume of MOF-5 was quite smaller in the work of Petit and Bandosz [27], however, the very small surface area and poor pore volume of the pristine MOF-5 makes it difficult to compare the results. It is speculated that the extended exposure to ammonia and significantly larger ammonia adsorption loadings on both MOFs have severely damaged the MOF frameworks.
## 3.3. X-ray diffraction patterns
X-ray diffraction patterns of MOF-5 and MOF-177 before and after exposure to ammonia are shown in Fig. 4. The XRD patterns of pure MOFs (a-MOF-5 and b-MOF-177) are very similar to those of previous reported in our earlier studies [7,8,10]. However, the XRD patterns of the resultant MOFs after exposure to ammonia appear to be quite different as indicated in Fig. 4c and d. In Fig. 4c, the largest peak of MOF-5 at around $10^{\circ}$ became very small in the ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ exposed MOF-5. There is a slight shift of the peak at $14^{\circ}$ to about $15.5^{\circ}$ in the exposed one. In the region of $19-35^{\circ}$ , there were several small peaks in the original MOF-5, however; only three small peaks are located in $32.8^{\circ}$ , $29^{\circ}$ and a splited peak at $25.2^{\circ}$ . The remaining large angle peaks of pure MOF-5 are disappeared. The changes in the peaks of MOF-5 in our study seem quite different than that reported by Petit and Bandosz [27]. In their study, except a splitting of the peak at $9.7^{\circ}$ , the overall pattern was observed to be identical even after exposure to $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ . Most probably, the exposure time was not high enough for the complete interference of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ with the adsorbent or the pristine species itself was not pure enough to observe the difference as there were several dissimilarities in XRD of pure MOF-5 reported by them and that of previously published results. In Fig. 4d, no peaks of pure MOF-177 was observed to remain after exposure to ammonia. Based on the patterns, it can be concluded that the original crystallinity of the structures were completely destroyed after they were exposed to ammonia.
## 3.4. FT-IR and Raman spectra
FT-IR spectra of MOF-5 and MOF-177 are given in Fig. 5. The asymmetric stretching of carboxylate group of BDC in MOF-5 (Fig. 5a) appears in around 1501 and $1588\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and symmetric stretching appeared around $1388\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ of the same material. Several bands are observed in the region of $1284{-}730\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which can be attributed to the out of plane vibration of BDC. In the FTIR of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ exposed MOF-5 (Fig. 5b), the peak positions remained almost identical with shifts no more than $\pm5\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for few peaks. The main difference between the peaks of pure and the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ exposed MOF-5 is that there is a sharp increase in peak intensities of all peaks in the spectrum of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ exposed MOF-5. The unaltered FTIR peak locations of MOF-5 were also reported by Petit and Bandosz [27], however, they did not mention about any change in the peak intensity. For MOF-177 (Fig. 5c), the peaks are quite similar because of the presence of a similar type of organic acid (BTB). The asymmetric stretching, symmetric stretching and out of plane vibrations appeared at $1536{-}1581\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1360\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and 1231– $700\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The changes in the FT-IR spectrum of $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ exposed MOF-177 (Fig. 5d) appears to be quite similar to that of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ exposed MOF-5; the peak locations are almost unaltered with a small increase in peak intensity.
The Raman spectra of MOF-5 and MOF-177 are given in Fig. 6. The noticeable shifts of MOF-5 (Fig. 6c) are at 1636, 1454, 1161 and $893~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The peak at $1636\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is probably attributed to the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ stretching of carboxylate group. The peaks at 1454 and $1161~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are generated from the in-plane vibrations of benzene ring and the peak at $893~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is probably caused by the out of plane deformation modes of $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bond. In the Raman mode of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ exposed MOF-5 (Fig. 6d), the peak locations are almost identical; however, a slight increase in intensity is of peaks is noticed.
In the Raman vibration of MOF-177 (Fig. 6a), there are number of peaks with high intensities are observed. The peak at $1632\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is probably originated from $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ stretching of carboxylate group. The other noticeable peaks appeared at 1372, 1265, 1171, 1023 (doublet) $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and a small peak at around $876~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . In ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ exposed MOF-177 (Fig. 6b), the peaks of the pure MOF-177 became much intense and two more peaks were noticed at 663 and $439\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ .
## 3.5. Chemical interference of ammonia with MOFs
Based on the FT-IR and Raman data, the general trend of shift of spectra is that there was an increase in the peak intensity in ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ exposed MOFs, either small or large. The peaks were contributed from the organic ligands present inside the MOFs as the metal part was not infrared or Raman sensitive. The increase in peak intensity suggests that the organic molecules inside the MOFs become more ‘free’ or more prone to the absorption of incident radiation. It is probably caused by the breaking of the linkage between the metal center and the organic ligand thereby generating the free organic molecules (linkers) within the species.
Observing the adsorption trend and materials characterizations after MOF-5 and MOF-177 were exposed to $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ , four similar phenomena have been noticed: (1) ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ adsorption amount in MOF-5 and MOF-177 are same after 150 torr pressure. (2) Drastic decrease in pore textural properties. (3) Loss of crystallinity. (4) Increase in peak intensity of FT-IR and Raman spectral bands. These observations made it quite clear that ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ molecules interfere chemically with these two MOFs thereby changing their structures. Below 150 torr partial pressure, the chemical interference of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ was not strong enough and therefore the adsorption amount was controlled by pore texture. It has also been confirmed by the higher uptake of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ by MOF-177 as that was having greater surface area and higher pore volume. After 150 torr pressure, ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ molecules started to interfere chemically with MOFs and deteriorated the linkage between the organic part and the metal centers. This deterioration invariably caused the destruction of crystal structure and thereby loss of pore textures. The generation of free organic molecules by the deterioration of bonds between the metal center and the organic ligand were confirmed by the increase peak intensity in FT-IR and Raman spectra. It can also be argued that the loss of structure of MOF-177 is more intense compared to that of MOF-5 as pore textures and XRD patterns of MOF-177 appears to be much more deteriorated compared to the original species.
Greathouse and Allendorf [30] and Li and Yang [31] proposed the reaction mechanisms of interference of water molecule with MOF-5 and MOF-177 respectively. They propose that water molecule forms a hydrogen bonding between hydrogen atom of water and oxygen atom of $Z\mathrm{n}_{4}\mathrm{O}$ cluster thereby altering the compositional identity of the MOFs. Based on this observation, Petit and Bandosz [27] suggested that ammonia molecule, being similar in chemical nature with water, forms the same type of hydrogen bonding with $Z\mathrm{n}_{4}0$ center and therefore destroys the MOF structure. Based on these observations, we proposed very similar reaction mechanisms for the chemical interference of $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ with Based in our observations, it can be concluded that both MOF-5 and MOF-177 that supports the formation of free organic ligands which seems quite probable according to the FT-IR and Raman spectral results.
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{{Zn}_{4}O(B D C)_{3}+4N H_{3}=[(Z n_{4}O)(N H_{3})_{4}(B D C)_{2}]^{2+}}}\\ {+\mathrm{{BDC^{2-}}(F o r~M O F-5)}}\end{array}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{BTB})_{2}+4\mathrm{NH}_{3}=[(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O})(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{4}(\mathrm{BTB})]^{3+}}\\ {+\mathrm{BTB}^{3-}(\mathrm{For~MOF}-177)}\end{array}
$$
## 4. Conclusions
Ammonia adsorption on MOF-5 and MOF-177 were performed at an ambient temperature and ammonia pressures up to 800 torr. At ammonia pressures below 150 torr, MOF-177 adsorbs more ammonia than MOF-5, but after which the adsorption isotherms were about the same for both MOFs, which suggests that a strong chemical interference mediated adsorption of $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ on the MOF frameworks occurred at an ammonia pressure of 150 torr. A drastic decrease in the pore textural properties was observed after both MOF-5 and MOF-177 were exposed to ammonia; the BET surface area decreased from 2449 to $10\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for MOF-5; and from 3275 to $4\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for MOF-177 accompanied by loss of the majority of pore volumes. XRD patterns suggested the loss crystallinity of MOF-5 and MOF-177 after the ammonia exposure. FT-IR and Raman studies indicated increases in peak intensity of organic species present in the MOFs after ammonia exposure, which was possibly caused by the formation of free organic molecules. The loss of MOF crystalline structures was probably caused by the formation of hydrogen bonding between the hydrogen atoms of ammonia and the oxygen atoms of $Z n_{4}0$ clusters, which is similar to what water molecules interfere with the MOF frameworks. Based on our observations, it can be concluded that both MOF-5 and MOF-177 are unsuitable for ammonia adsorption because ammonia will destroy the MOF frameworks.
